    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2HHkasK1mnHK76yFvmtt9Jo)_\n\n**Cause.** `org-table-widget` hides each row's source and draws the row as the `before-string` of an overlay. A click or `RET` is delivered to the hidden source, where `org-open-at-point` finds no link, climbs to the headline and offers every link of the entry. That is the menu.\n\n**Fix** (in `sp-org-tables`, literate doc updated):\n- A click follows the link under the pointer (advice on `org-open-at-mouse`, reading the `htmlize-link` property the widget keeps in its cells).\n- `RET` (in `org-table-widget-map`) and `C-c C-o` on a row follow its link; a chooser appears only when the row has several links, since `RET` cannot know which was meant. A row without links keeps the old `RET` (`org-return`).\n\n**Tested.** 5 ERT tests with a faked widget row, Emacs 29.3 batch (`tests/sp-org-tables-links-test.el`), and the tangle check. **Not run** with the real widget in a graphical Emacs (also the layout preserving `htmlize-link` across wrapped cells, which I read in the source but did not see run).\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01WWLNiB6Wmur34ZgK1uipCm)_",
